    {
      "id": "MJUe",
      "code_hash": "713cd6b5193bda8f811464aac72af55c",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><h1 id=\"a-verified-fitness-pipeline\">A verified fitness pipeline</h1>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">A watch streams heart-rate samples, and users log workouts in an app. For\ntraining a model we want <strong>the heart rate during each workout</strong>, with\nsensor glitches removed. The pipeline is a few lines of Ibis; the\ninteresting part is the <em>contract</em> on it:</span>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>requires</strong>: what we assume about the inputs (at most one sample per\n  user and timestamp; every workout has a start and an end);</li>\n<li><strong>ensures</strong>: what the output must satisfy (plausible heart rates, no\n  NaN, every sample inside its workout, one row per user and timestamp).</li>\n</ul>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">Lean checks every <code>ensures</code> against the pipeline's Substrait plan <em>before\nany data runs</em>. A \u2705 is a proof for <strong>all</strong> possible inputs that satisfy\n<code>requires</code>, not a test on the rows below. Then the same pipeline runs as\nan incrementally maintained view whose update rules are proved correct\ntoo.</span></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
    {
      "id": "vblA",
      "code_hash": "b553a633cedfa49fb77e3540d4a5a990",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><h2 id=\"1-the-data\">1. The data</h2>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">Real sensor data is messy: <code>-1</code> means the sensor lost contact, and the\noccasional <code>NaN</code> slips through. User 2's 09:00 sample is outside any\nworkout.</span></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
